            <ArticleTitle>Acute pain and opioid seeking behaviour.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1009-12</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Acute pain is a common presentation associated with opioid seeking behaviour.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This case study provides a practical approach for general practitioners seeing patients with acute pain whom they suspect of seeking opioids because of dependence.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Acute pain commonly presents as an emergency appointment 'squeezed in' between booked appointments. General practitioners have to make a rapid assessment of the possible underlying causes, relieve pain, and establish a plan for further investigation and management. Furthermore, some opioid dependent people can and do effectively feign acute pain in order to obtain opioid medication.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
